  "explanation": "The proposal combines multi-agent LLM dialogue for research ideation with latent activation probing to steer generation away from medium-novelty traps. While multi-agent ideation and literature-grounded generation have been heavily explored (e.g., SciMON, Virtual-Scientists, and multi-agent critique loops), and latent novelty probing was recently introduced for standalone judges (ThinkProbe-Respond), integrating activation steering into a resource-constrained multi-agent generation loop is a moderately novel combination.",
  "problems": [
    "The core concept of probing hidden states for novelty builds directly on the recent ThinkProbe-Respond method, limiting the originality of the probing mechanism itself.",
    "The assumption that linear probes trained on standalone judgment tasks transfer effectively to multi-agent generation-steering trajectories without fine-tuning requires stronger validation.",
    "Layer-selective activation extraction and caching mechanisms on quantized 8B models under a strict 12GB VRAM constraint may introduce latency and memory bottlenecks during real-time multi-agent loops."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Clarify how the linear probe trained on auxiliary judgment data generalizes to dynamic, multi-turn generation revisions where token distributions differ from standalone evaluation.",
    "Provide preliminary profiling metrics or memory budgets confirming that simultaneous residual stream extraction, caching, and generation fit within 12GB VRAM without severe speed degradation.",
    "Differentiate the proposed contrastive prompt mapping strategy more clearly from standard retrieval-augmented critique prompting."
  ],
